From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 19:36:53 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: add the USB0 controller and PHY

usb1 was described alone because the only board in the tree at the time
was a FRITZ!Box 7560, whose vendor board file disables usb0, and a
controller no board could enable would have been dead DT. The FRITZ!Box
7590 makes that reasoning obsolete: AVM's HW226 board files disable
neither controller, and its SoC-level nodes for both are enabled
(AVM 4.9 arch/mips/boot/dts/lantiq/xrx500.dtsi). Two USB ports is one of
the differences between the two boards.

So describe usb0 as well, disabled like its sibling and enabled by
whichever board file wires it. Its numbers are the vendor's, and they
are the same numbers the tree already carried in prose in the block
comment: core at 0x1a300000, PHY at 0x1a400000, GIC_SHARED 54 for the
core, RCU reset bank 1 bit 4, chip-top configuration word at +0x18,
GCLK_USB0 for the gate clock. GIC_SHARED 54 is not otherwise claimed in
this dtsi.

Bank 1 bit 4 and the +0x18 word have independent corroboration beyond
the vendor tree. Documentation/devicetree/bindings/mips/lantiq/rcu.txt
documents the xRX200's two USB PHYs with the same pairing -- bank 1 bit
4 with the config word at +0x18, bank 1 bit 5 with the word at +0x34 --
which is the split this SoC keeps, with the word moved from the RCU to
the chip-top syscon.

The block comment needed correcting rather than extending. It asserted
that "the vendor tree disables usb0 on all of them" and that "its pins
are not bonded out to anything": the first is now false, and the second
was never sourced -- AVM's evidence only ever showed the glue node
disabled on the 7560, with usb0_phy left enabled, which says nothing
about bonding. It also explained the absent over-current interrupt by
naming the DECT and WLAN buttons, which is the FRITZ!Box 7560's pinout;
on the 7590 pad 2 is the WLAN button but pad 7 is CLKOUT1. The
conclusion holds on both boards -- no board routes over-current, which
is why the vendor deletes all four pinctrl_usb* nodes -- but the stated
reason did not generalise, so it now names both boards.

The same unsourced bonding claim in the FRITZ!Box 7560 board file is
corrected the same way: usb0 is disabled there because the vendor
disables it, and what its pins reach is unknown.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
index 3c4d9d9673ad..6cda1e5d7b0d 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/xrx500.dtsi
@@ -394,6 +394,31 @@ pcie2: pcie@18e00000 {
 	 * word; intel,syscon's argument is that controller's AXI
 	 * configuration offset.
 	 */
+	usb0_phy: phy@1a400000 {
+		compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy";
+		reg = <0x1a400000 0x10000>;
+		#phy-cells = <0>;
+		clocks = <&cgu GCLK_USB0>;
+		resets = <&rcu_reset1 4 4>;
+		reset-names = "phy";
+		intel,syscon = <&chiptop 0x18>;
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
+	usb0: usb@1a300000 {
+		compatible = "snps,dwc3";
+		reg = <0x1a300000 0x10000>;
+
+		interrupt-parent = <&gic>;
+		interrupts = <GIC_SHARED 54 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+
+		dr_mode = "host";
+		snps,dis_rxdet_inp3_quirk;
+		phys = <&usb0_phy>;
+		phy-names = "usb3-phy";
+		status = "disabled";
+	};
+
 	usb1_phy: phy@1a600000 {
 		compatible = "intel,grx500-usb-phy";
 		reg = <0x1a600000 0x10000>;
